faq:
  - q: "What should a LinkedIn summary include?"
    a: "A strong LinkedIn summary (the About section) has four parts: (1) Who you are professionally — your level, function, and domain. (2) What you have accomplished — 2–3 specific outcomes with numbers. (3) What you specialize in — the intersection of your strongest skills and your most interesting problems. (4) What you are looking for — give recruiters a reason and direction to reach out. The best summaries are 150–250 words: long enough to establish credibility, short enough to be read."
  - q: "How do I write a LinkedIn About section?"
    a: "Start with your professional identity in the first sentence — this is what appears before 'see more.' Then describe your specialization and strongest outcomes. End with a targeting statement. Use short paragraphs or bullets — not one block of text. Write in first person. Do not open with 'I am a passionate professional.'"
  - q: "What is the LinkedIn summary formula?"
    a: "[Level + Role] at [company type or scale] → [domain specialty] → [signature outcome or result] → [what you are targeting or open to]. Example: 'Staff Software Engineer specializing in distributed systems and platform infrastructure at Series B through public-company scale. Built the event streaming backbone at [Company] that processed 4M events/day with 99.99% uptime. Now exploring Staff+ and engineering leadership roles at mission-driven tech companies.'"
  - q: "How long should a LinkedIn summary be?"
    a: "150–300 words is ideal. LinkedIn shows only the first ~220 characters before truncating — so your hook must land in the first sentence. Below 100 words signals a low-effort profile. Above 400 words loses readers."
  - q: "Should I use bullet points in my LinkedIn summary?"
    a: "Optional — but effective for readability. If your most important information is buried in a paragraph, bullets help it surface. Use a hybrid: 1–2 sentences of identity framing, then 3–4 bullets of key outcomes or specialties, then a closing targeting statement."
